  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, light rain showers will continue this\nafternoon before ending from west to east for areas south of US\nHighway 2. As we head into tonight, expect overnight drizzle and\npatchy fog to develop. This could keep some surfaces wet and slick\ninto early Saturday morning before slowly ending during the mid to\nlate morning hours tomorrow. Aside from the rain showers today,\ndrizzle tonight, and some inland northern Wisconsin rain showers\ntomorrow, it is a dry forecast for the region.\n\nGusty easterly winds will continue into the southwest arm of Lake\nSuperior this weekend, affecting beaches in the Twin Ports with\ndangerous rip currents. While the ongoing Beach Hazard Statement for\ntoday will expire tonight, expect a few more days of these similar\nheadlines this weekend and into at least Monday.\n\nOn Sunday, strong easterly winds are forecast for the southwest arm\nand the South Shore. This setup has a 40 percent chance of bringing\n35 to 40 mph wind gusts to shoreline locations during the daytime.\nWhile this is not enough for a Wind Advisory, it will be gusty for\nany outdoor weekend plans. High pressure will begin to settle over\nthe area keeping precipitation chances near zero.\n\nAs we look toward the extended forecast, frost is increasingly likely\nfor cold spots of inland northwest Wisconsin and far northern\nMinnesota late Sunday night into Monday morning. Frost Advisories may\nbe needed for that time period, but a zone or two of Freeze Warnings\ncould also be warranted for areas along the International Border if\nskies can clear out.\n\nThe forecast trends into show continued cool nights Monday \nnight and Tuesday night so even additional at least frost and \nmaybe freeze headlines are likely. High pressure will remain in\ncontrol over the Great Lakes region, keeping our weather mostly\nclear and quiet from Tuesday through Thursday. High \ntemperatures will generally hover in the upper 50s to middle 60s\nthroughout the week.",
